Reinforcement learning receives neither targets nor data alone, but rewards for its actions; 

Losses and Performance Measures describes how the reward function serves as its performance measure. One prominent use connects it with language models. In reinforcement learning from human feedback, people rank several answers of a model, a reward model learns these preferences, and reinforcement learning then tunes the language model to produce answers with a high reward (Ouyang et al., see 

Further Reading).

Sentiment analysis decides whether a text expresses a positive, negative, or neutral opinion; finer variants recognize emotions such as anger, fear, joy, or surprise. Companies use it to monitor reviews and social media, and platforms use it to moderate content. Many statements are easy, because a few words carry the sentiment: “I like this product” is positive, “The food was really bad” negative, “I was going to the town” neutral. Others are hard:

negation: “I can’t say I liked it” contains the positive word “liked”;

unclear polarity: “Drinking wine is not my thing” may be negative or merely a preference;

sarcasm: “What a fine artist you’ve become!” may mean the opposite of its words;

multiple negations: “I haven’t ever owed anything to anyone” is full of negative words but positive in meaning.

Classifiers that treat a text as a bag of words cannot resolve these cases; models that read words in context can, at least in part.

Key Formula: Masked Self-Attention\text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V}) = \operatorname{softmax}\!\left(\frac{\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{K}^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}} + \mathbf{M}\right) \mathbf{V}

The mask \mathbf{M} is zero for encoders and -\infty above the diagonal for decoders, which see only the past.

Key Formula: LoRA\mathbf{W} = \mathbf{W}_0 + \mathbf{B}\mathbf{A}, \qquad r \ll d

A frozen pretrained matrix plus a trainable update of low rank r.

Key Formula: Temperature SamplingP_\tau(w) = \frac{\exp(z_w / \tau)}{\sum_{v} \exp(z_v / \tau)}

A temperature below 1 sharpens the next-token distribution, above 1 flattens it; top-p sampling then keeps the most probable tokens that cover the probability p.

The direct evaluation method follows the initial intuition. For each document D_i, the system checks whether every atomic predicate \tau_{l,k} is true or false and then evaluates the complete expression. For cat AND dog, D_1 produces \text{true} ∧ \text{true} = \text{true}, whereas D_4 produces \text{true} ∧ \text{false} = \text{false}.

A second method starts from all documents that satisfy each atomic predicate. Let \mathbb{S}_t denote the set of documents containing term t. For the running collection:

\mathbb{S}_{\text{cat}} = \{D_1,D_4,D_9,D_{10},D_{12}\}

\mathbb{S}_{\text{dog}} = \{D_1,D_3,D_7,D_9,D_{10},D_{12}\}

The documents satisfying cat AND dog must belong to both sets. The documents satisfying cat OR dog may belong to either set:

\mathbb{S}_{\text{cat}} ∩ \mathbb{S}_{\text{dog}} = \{D_1,D_9,D_{10},D_{12}\}

\mathbb{S}_{\text{cat}} \cup \mathbb{S}_{\text{dog}} = \{D_1,D_3,D_4,D_7,D_9,D_{10},D_{12}\}

More generally, each atomic predicate defines a set of matching documents:

\mathbb{S}_{l,k} = \begin{cases} \{D_i ∣ \text{tf}(D_i, t_{j(l,k)}) \geq 1\} & \text{if } \tau_{l,k} = t_{j(l,k)} \\ \{D_i ∣ \text{tf}(D_i, t_{j(l,k)}) = 0\} & \text{if } \tau_{l,k} = ¬ t_{j(l,k)}. \end{cases}

The DNF structure translates directly into intersections for AND and unions for OR:

\mathbb{Q} = ⋃_{l=1}^{L} ⋂_{k=1}^{K_l} \mathbb{S}_{l,k}

An inverted file stores these term-document sets so that the system can combine them without scanning every document. Chapter 4 develops this index structure and its efficient query-processing algorithms.

The BIR model uses the same tokenized vocabulary as the previous models, but represents each query and document as a set of terms. It was formalized by Robertson and Spärck Jones; see 

Relevance Weighting of Search Terms in the Further Reading section of the chapter summary. A component is 1 when a term is present and 0 when it is absent. It assumes that terms contribute independently and that non-query terms occur equally often in relevant and non-relevant documents. Under these assumptions, only query terms present in a document affect its rank.

Let R denote relevance and NR non-relevance for query Q. Ranking by the posterior odds\frac{P(R ∣ D_i,Q)}{P(NR ∣ D_i,Q)}

is equivalent to ranking by the document evidence after query-dependent constants are removed. Definer_j=P(d_{i,j}=1 ∣ R,Q), \qquad n_j=P(d_{i,j}=1 ∣ NR,Q).

The first probability measures how often term t_j occurs in relevant documents; the second measures how often it occurs in non-relevant documents. The resulting score is additive, but several probability terms must cancel before we reach that form. The derivation below explains why only query terms present in the document remain.

From posterior odds to the BIR score (optional reading)

Bayes’ rule separates the posterior odds into prior odds and document evidence:\frac{P(R ∣ \mathbf{d}_i,Q)}{P(NR ∣ \mathbf{d}_i,Q)}
=
\frac{P(R ∣ Q)}{P(NR ∣ Q)}
\cdot
\frac{P(\mathbf{d}_i ∣ R,Q)}{P(\mathbf{d}_i ∣ NR,Q)}.

The prior odds P(R ∣ Q)/P(NR ∣ Q) are the same for every document considered for query Q. They affect the numerical score but not the ranking. We can therefore focus on the likelihood ratio containing the document evidence.

Under the binary representation, each component satisfies d_{i,j}\in\{0,1\}: it is 1 when document D_i contains term t_j and 0 otherwise. Combined with term independence, this makes the probability of the complete document vector a product of Bernoulli probabilities. The exponents select the appropriate case: r_j^{d_{i,j}} contributes r_j when the term is present, while (1-r_j)^{1-d_{i,j}} contributes 1-r_j when it is absent.P(\mathbf{d}_i ∣ R,Q)
=
\prod_{j=1}^{M} r_j^{d_{i,j}}(1-r_j)^{1-d_{i,j}},P(\mathbf{d}_i ∣ NR,Q)
=
\prod_{j=1}^{M} n_j^{d_{i,j}}(1-n_j)^{1-d_{i,j}}.

Substituting these products into the odds and taking the logarithm turns multiplication into addition:\log\frac{P(R ∣ \mathbf{d}_i,Q)}{P(NR ∣ \mathbf{d}_i,Q)}
=
\log\frac{P(R ∣ Q)}{P(NR ∣ Q)}
+
\sum_{j=1}^{M}
\left[
 d_{i,j}\log\frac{r_j}{n_j}
 +(1-d_{i,j})\log\frac{1-r_j}{1-n_j}
\right].

For a term absent from the query, BIR assumes r_j=n_j. Both logarithms then become zero, so every non-query term disappears. For a query term, we can rearrange its contribution as\log\frac{1-r_j}{1-n_j}
+
d_{i,j}\log\frac{r_j(1-n_j)}{n_j(1-r_j)}.

The first part depends on the query but not on the document, so it is another constant that does not change the ranking. The second part contributes only when d_{i,j}=1. After removing all ranking constants, the remaining coefficient isc_j=\log\frac{r_j(1-n_j)}{n_j(1-r_j)},

which produces the additive BIR score below.

Key Formula: BIR Similarity\text{sim}_{\text{BIR}}(Q,D_i)=\sum_{j:\,q_j=1,\,d_{i,j}=1}c_j, \qquad c_j=\log\frac{r_j(1-n_j)}{n_j(1-r_j)}

A positive c_j means that term t_j is more characteristic of relevant than non-relevant documents. Documents receive the sum of the weights for query terms they contain.

Lucene models a document as a set of named fields, and not every document needs every field. A field carries a name, a value, and a type that specifies whether the value is tokenized, what is written to the inverted index, and whether the original value is stored for retrieval. These three choices are independent: a long body field is typically tokenized and indexed for search but not stored, so the application fetches the original text from its own database. Field subclasses cover the common cases: a text field is tokenized and indexed with term frequencies and positions; string and numeric fields are indexed without tokenization for exact-match and range queries on metadata; a stored-only field is kept but not searchable.

Fields are indexed independently. If both a title field and a body field contain “house”, Lucene treats the two occurrences as distinct terms, internally prefixed as “title:house” and “body:house”. This is what lets a search weight a title match more heavily than a body match, or apply different normalization to each field.

Tokenization is the job of an analyzer, and the choice of analyzer must be identical for indexing and for querying. The standard analyzer lowercases and strips punctuation; the English analyzer additionally removes stop words and applies a Porter stemmer; custom analyzers can combine filters such as stemming, length limits, and case handling. Changing the analyzer means rebuilding the index.// Comparing analyzer output for the same text
var text = "I think text's values' color goes here; WHAT happens ...";

// StandardAnalyzer: lowercase, strip punctuation, keep possessives
print_tokens(new StandardAnalyzer(), text);
// -> i think text's values color goes here what happens ...

// EnglishAnalyzer: + stop-word removal + Porter stemming
print_tokens(new EnglishAnalyzer(), text);
// -> think text valu color goe here what happen ...

// Custom stop words
var stopWords = new CharArraySet(Arrays.asList("i", "do"), false);
print_tokens(new EnglishAnalyzer(stopWords), text);
// -> think text valu color goe here what happen ...

Lucene writes the index as a set of immutable segments. Each time an IndexWriter flushes, it creates a new segment, which reduces contention and protects against corruption. Because segments are immutable, Lucene never edits a document in place, and it has no primary key: internally a document is known only by a docId that can change when segments merge. Deletes and updates therefore identify documents by content. A delete-by-term marks every document whose chosen key field holds a given value, for example id:"B-10432", by flipping a bit in the segment’s live-documents list rather than erasing any data; a delete-by-query does the same for documents matching an arbitrary query. An update is a delete-by-term of the key value followed by an add of the new version, applied atomically. A configurable merge policy periodically combines smaller segments into larger ones, and only during a merge are the documents flagged as deleted physically dropped. Searching runs over all live segments in parallel and merges their results, which is the seed of the distributed scaling in the next section.

The following shows how documents are built and added to an index:// Set up analyzer, directory, and writer
Analyzer analyzer = new EnglishAnalyzer();
Directory directory = FSDirectory.open(Paths.get("./index"));
IndexWriter writer = new IndexWriter(directory, new IndexWriterConfig(analyzer));

// Build a document from application data
Document doc = new Document();
doc.add(new TextField("title", "Star Wars", Field.Store.YES));
doc.add(new IntField("year", 1977, Field.Store.YES));
doc.add(new TextField("body", "A long time ago ...", Field.Store.NO));
writer.addDocument(doc);

// Close the writer to flush a new segment
writer.close();

Each TextField is tokenized and indexed; the IntField supports exact and range queries on metadata. Setting Store.NO on the body means its text is searchable but not retrievable from results, keeping the stored-fields footprint small.

When a new document \mathbf{d} arrives (represented as a term vector in the original m-dimensional space), we project it into the k-dimensional latent space without recomputing the full SVD (see 

Figure 3):

Key Formula: LSI Projection\bar{\mathbf{d}}^\top = \mathbf{d}^\top \mathbf{U}_k \mathbf{S}_k^{-1}

A new document (or query) vector \mathbf{d} is mapped to the latent topic space by multiplying with \mathbf{U}_k \mathbf{S}_k^{-1}. This is valid as long as the new document does not significantly alter the collection’s topic structure.



Figure 3:Derivation of the folding-in formula in three steps: transpose the truncated SVD equation, multiply by the pseudo-inverse, and isolate a single document vector.

Queries are treated as short documents. The query vector \mathbf{q} (with non-zero entries for query terms) is projected identically:\bar{\mathbf{q}}^\top = \mathbf{q}^\top \mathbf{U}_k \mathbf{S}_k^{-1}

Retrieval then computes cosine similarity between the projected query and all projected documents:\text{sim}_{\cos}(Q, D_i) = \frac{\bar{\mathbf{q}} \cdot \bar{\mathbf{d}}_i}{\|\bar{\mathbf{q}}\| \cdot \|\bar{\mathbf{d}}_i\|}

Lucene stores vectors as one more field type of a document. The document of 

Practical Frameworks receives a KnnFloatVectorField next to its text and numeric fields:// 1. A movie document with text, numeric, and vector fields
Document doc = new Document();
doc.add(new TextField("title", "Inception", Field.Store.YES));
doc.add(new TextField("actors", "Leonardo DiCaprio, Joseph Gordon-Levitt", Field.Store.NO));
doc.add(new TextField("genres", "Thriller, Science Fiction", Field.Store.NO));
doc.add(new IntField("year", 2010, Field.Store.YES));
doc.add(new FloatField("rating", 8.8f, Field.Store.YES));
doc.add(new KnnFloatVectorField("embedding", embedding,     // float[1024]
    VectorSimilarityFunction.COSINE));
writer.addDocument(doc);

// 2. Approximate k-NN query on the vector field
float[] query = embed("a mind-bending heist thriller");
Query knn = new KnnFloatVectorQuery("embedding", query, 10);
TopDocs top = searcher.search(knn, 10);

Lucene builds an HNSW graph for the vector field of every segment. A query searches the graph of each segment and merges the per-segment results, the same scatter-gather pattern as for text queries. Because segments are merged in the background, their graphs must be merged as well, which makes indexing vectors considerably more expensive than indexing text. Lucene reports cosine similarity as a score in [0, 1], computed as (1 + \cos)/2, so that higher always means better. For lever 2, Lucene offers vector formats that quantize the stored vectors to 7 or 4 bits per component, or to a single bit. The full vectors stay in the index, so the best candidates can be rescored with them: since version 10.3, the RescoreTopNQuery does this inside Lucene; in earlier versions, the application rescores.

OpenSearch wraps Lucene in a distributed engine, as described in 

Scaling Out. Vector search is a field type of the index mapping. The knn_vector field declares the dimensions, the similarity, and the ANN method:# 1. Index mapping: text fields, numeric fields, and a vector field
client.indices.create(index="movies", body={
    "settings": {"index": {"knn": True}},
    "mappings": {"properties": {
        "title":   {"type": "text"},
        "actors":  {"type": "text"},
        "genres":  {"type": "text"},
        "year":    {"type": "integer"},
        "rating":  {"type": "float"},
        "embedding": {
            "type": "knn_vector",
            "dimension": 1024,
            "space_type": "cosinesimil",
            "method": {"name": "hnsw", "engine": "faiss",
                       "parameters": {"m": 16, "ef_construction": 128}},
        },
    }},
})

# 2. Approximate k-NN query
query = embed("a mind-bending heist thriller")
response = client.search(index="movies", body={
    "size": 10,
    "query": {"knn": {"embedding": {"vector": query, "k": 10}}},
})

The engine parameter selects the implementation behind the field. The Lucene engine uses Lucene’s own HNSW graphs; the FAISS engine runs FAISS inside every shard and also offers IVF and product quantization. The parameters m and ef_construction are the HNSW parameters of the previous section. A query is sent to every shard, each shard searches its own index and returns its own top k, and a coordinating node merges the shard results. The global top k is always among the per-shard lists, so merging adds no error of its own; the approximation comes only from the ANN search inside each shard. The price is that every query must visit every shard.

FAISS has no metadata, so the application evaluates the filter on its own data and passes the admitted ids to the search. An IDSelector makes the index skip all other ids while it scans the probed lists, which is filtering during the search. With a selective filter, the probed lists may contain fewer than ten admitted movies, so it pays to raise n_{\text{probe}} or to switch to an exact scan over the admitted vectors. FAISS has no keyword search either. A separate engine computes the BM25 ranking, and the application fuses the two rankings:# 1. Filter in the application, then search only the admitted ids
admitted = movies.query("year > 2005 and rating >= 7.5")["id"].to_numpy()
params = faiss.SearchParametersIVF(sel=faiss.IDSelectorBatch(admitted), nprobe=32)
_, dense_ids = index.search(q, 50, params=params)

# 2. BM25 ranking from a keyword engine, restricted to the same ids
sparse_ids = bm25_search('actors:"leonardo dicaprio" genres:thriller', admitted, 50)

# 3. Reciprocal rank fusion of the two rankings
def rrf(*rankings, k=60):
    scores = {}
    for ranking in rankings:
        for rank, doc_id in enumerate(ranking, start=1):
            scores[doc_id] = scores.get(doc_id, 0.0) + 1.0 / (k + rank)
    return sorted(scores, key=scores.get, reverse=True)

top10 = rrf(dense_ids[0], sparse_ids)[:10]

Lucene filters during the search. The filter is passed as the fourth argument of KnnFloatVectorQuery. Lucene evaluates it per segment and searches the graph only for admitted documents. If fewer documents pass the filter than the query asks for, it runs an exact search instead, and if the graph search visits too many vectors without completing, it stops and switches to an exact search as well. The keyword part becomes ordinary BM25 clauses of a Boolean query:// 1. Filter on year and rating (FILTER clauses do not score)
Query filter = new BooleanQuery.Builder()
    .add(IntField.newRangeQuery("year", 2006, Integer.MAX_VALUE), Occur.FILTER)
    .add(FloatField.newRangeQuery("rating", 7.5f, Float.POSITIVE_INFINITY), Occur.FILTER)
    .build();

// 2. Vector query that applies the filter during the graph search
Query knn = new KnnFloatVectorQuery("embedding", query, 50, filter);

// 3. Hybrid query: vector score + BM25 on actors and genres
Query hybrid = new BooleanQuery.Builder()
    .add(knn, Occur.SHOULD)
    .add(new PhraseQuery("actors", "leonardo", "dicaprio"), Occur.SHOULD)
    .add(new TermQuery(new Term("genres", "thriller")), Occur.SHOULD)
    .add(filter, Occur.FILTER)
    .build();
TopDocs top = searcher.search(hybrid, 10);

Two details matter here. First, the filter belongs inside the vector query. Adding it only as a FILTER clause of the Boolean query, next to an unfiltered KnnFloatVectorQuery, runs the two parts independently: the vector query returns its top 50 over all movies, and the Boolean query intersects them with the filter. This is post-filtering, with all its risks. Second, a Boolean query adds the scores of its clauses. The vector clause contributes at most 1, while a BM25 clause for a rare name can contribute several points, so the keyword part tends to dominate. Lucene itself provides no rank fusion; applications either weight the clauses with boosts or run the two queries separately and fuse their rankings with RRF.

OpenSearch passes a filter inside the knn clause to the engine, which then filters during the search in the same way as Lucene. For the keyword part, a hybrid query runs its sub-queries separately, and a search pipeline fuses their results. Since version 2.19, the score-ranker-processor implements RRF:# 1. Search pipeline that fuses the sub-query rankings with RRF
client.transport.perform_request("PUT", "/_search/pipeline/rrf-pipeline", body={
    "phase_results_processors": [{"score-ranker-processor": {
        "combination": {"technique": "rrf", "rank_constant": 60}}}],
})

# 2. Hybrid query: BM25 on actors and genres, k-NN on the overview,
#    both restricted by the same filter on year and rating
filters = {"bool": {"filter": [
    {"range": {"year": {"gt": 2005}}},
    {"range": {"rating": {"gte": 7.5}}},
]}}
response = client.search(index="movies", params={"search_pipeline": "rrf-pipeline"},
    body={"size": 10, "query": {"hybrid": {
        "queries": [
            {"bool": {"should": [
                {"match_phrase": {"actors": "Leonardo DiCaprio"}},
                {"match": {"genres": "thriller"}},
            ]}},
            {"knn": {"embedding": {"vector": query, "k": 50}}},
        ],
        "filter": filters,
    }}})

The filter of the hybrid query applies to both sub-queries. Instead of RRF, the pipeline can also use a normalization-processor, which rescales the scores of each sub-query to a common range and then combines them with weights. This keeps more information than ranks alone, at the cost of choosing a normalization and the weights.

The methods above each pick one chunk size, and that single choice cannot serve both retrieval and generation well. Hierarchical chunking refuses the choice and stores the document at more than one level of granularity at once. This is the arrangement most suited to RAG.

The idea is to split coarsely, then finely, and keep both. Divide the document by large structural units such as headings or sections. Within each unit, apply a finer splitter: sentence-based, fixed-token, or semantic. Store both levels, and give each a job. Small chunks produce accurate embeddings and drive retrieval; the larger section they belong to is passed to the language model as context, so generation sees the full surrounding passage even though retrieval matched on a precise fragment.from langchain_text_splitters import (
    MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter,
    RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter,
)

header_splitter = MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter(
    headers_to_split_on=[("#", "H1"), ("##", "H2"), ("###", "H3")]
)
sections = header_splitter.split_text(long_markdown_text)

token_splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=512, chunk_overlap=50)
final_chunks = []
for sec in sections:
    for idx, sub in enumerate(token_splitter.split_text(sec.page_content)):
        final_chunks.append({
            "content": sub,
            "metadata": {**sec.metadata, "sub_chunk": idx},
        })

Retrieval runs against the small sub chunks; the stored metadata lets the system recover the parent section and hand that larger unit to the generator.

The random jump in PageRank picks every page with the same probability. Nothing forces this choice. If the surfer always jumps back to a fixed set of pages \mathbb{S}, the stationary distribution measures importance from the point of view of \mathbb{S}: pages that are close to \mathbb{S} in the link graph and reachable through many paths receive high scores. This variant is called Personalized PageRank. Formally, we replace the uniform jump vector \frac{1}{N} \cdot \mathbf{1} with a jump distribution \mathbf{s} that is non-zero only on \mathbb{S}:\mathbf{r} = (1 - \alpha) \cdot \mathbf{s} + \alpha \cdot \mathbf{M} \mathbf{r}

With \mathbb{S} chosen as the pages of one topic, for example all pages that a web directory lists under “sports”, we obtain a topic-specific importance score. Haveliwala’s topic-sensitive PageRank precomputes one such vector per topic and mixes them at query time according to the topic of the query. With \mathbb{S} chosen as a single node, for example one user in a social network, Personalized PageRank ranks all other nodes by how closely connected they are to that user. This is the basis for many “people you may know” and “who to follow” recommendations, which we revisit in 

Ranking Without a Query: Feeds.
